      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I\u0027m going to more strongly object to this entire approach, as I have in the past.\n\nI thought you suggested this as an alternative approach in https://skia-review.googlesource.com/c/skia/+/1008277/3#message-68e514bfd2b5587a31d409c7903e2be6f4cdc863, what did you mean by \"having a //:pathops (which depends on //:skia) target that //:skia doesn\u0027t directly depend on, but //:pdf and //:xps do depend on only when enabled\"?\n\nAlso this is not that different from https://skia-review.googlesource.com/c/skia/+/1009616 in terms of skia/pdf partitioning.\n\n\u003e The code in pdf/ and xps/ logically cannot exist outside of libskia. These work by implementing SkDevice and (must) use a ridiculous number of internal types which will never (or at least should never) be public. The pdf/ and xps/ code, if used, must be in the skia target. For example this CL passes the \"Shared\" builds simply by making it impossible to create an official (or any) shared build of pdf/ and xps/ at all (either in libskia.so or on their own).\n\nI think that makes sense.  It also points to pdf not being used currently in our Shared test builds.  If we\u0027re claiming to support component builds, we should prolly build something that links against libskia.\n\n\u003e In fact it seems this change makes it impossible (especially for outside users of Skia) to build pdf/ and xps/ in the GN build unless statically linking Skia. Linkers do not enforce any visibility on static objects.\n\nAll the clients I know of have their own build files and don\u0027t rely on our top level GN at all. They make their own shared lib boundary decisions (e.g. Chromium rolls PDF, Skottie, etc. into libskia.so), and that will continue to work fine.\n\nThe decision to include PDF/XPS support seems arbitrary (why not SVG, etc?), what will break if we change our mind?\n\n\u003e The proper way to do any of this is to make an interface for pathops and make it plug-able. Even if there is only ever one implementation this is still what needs to be done.\n\nAnd how will the pathops impl link against libskia?  Pull a different skia copy?  What\u0027s the point of using a shared lib then?\n\n\u003e Or we publicly state that Skia\u0027s build will no longer support building shared objects (which would be compatible with the Bazel build), that the Skia build will produce only static libraries, and then the user (and maybe a Skia side helper) can gather up all these static libraries into a libskia shared library? This is essentially just acknowledging the \"big ball of mud\".\n\nOr we only drop PDF support from libskia.so, and keep the rest of core.  I don\u0027t see why this has to be \"pdf or nothing\".\n\nBut yeah, I think this is where we are today, effectively.  All the clients we support manage their own balls of mud -- are there any external users who happen to be perfectly aligned with our libskia.so choices and use it as-is?\n\n\u003eInternally everything can have full visibility into everything else (because the linker does not enforce visibility on static libraries), but can still be separate targets. This is essentially what the Bazel build is doing (Bazel enforces through its own \"visibility\", not linker visibility), except we could make it useful by then having a \"skia library\" target which pulls in a bunch of static libraries and builds a shared library out of them (at which point the linker will finally export the visibility information). This is more or less what the GN //:skia component is, except assembled from source_set instead of static_library. Doing something like this would allow a change like this to land without breaking users who want pdf/ in a libskia.so\n\nAre there interesting differences between source_set and static_library in this context?  I thought all static_library does additionally is generate the archive (which is prolly unnecessary), but it still has deps etc.  The main issue is the :pathops -\u003e :skia dependency, which I assume would still be problematic with source_set?",
